      "writtenOn": "2017-01-11T20:45:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These are some intense assertions. We could improve readability (and reviewablility!) by breaking them into their own well-named variables.",
      "revId": "06ef92126debd9d4c330890f1abae15e32bdcd4c",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ba5201f7_a13eba88",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/test_v3_filters.py",
        "patchSetId": 33
      },
      "lineNbr": 361,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-11T20:45:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A personal goal of mine when coming up with unit tests names (which I\u0027m terrible at) is to follow a convention I stumbled across in The Art of Unit Testing [1]. I find it useful because it provides reviewers and readers of the test with a bunch of information, just from the test name, which means it\u0027s available in test output, etc... The only downside is that sometimes the names get a bit long, in which case I find it easier to condense the test name to a simpler version of the same message and provide more detail in a doc string.\n\n  def test_{thing_under_test}_{condition}_{result}:\n  def test_list_users_by_password_expires_with_bad_operator_fails(self):\n\n\n[0] http://osherove.com/blog/2005/4/3/naming-standards-for-unit-tests.html\n[1] https://www.manning.com/books/the-art-of-unit-testing-second-edition",
      "revId": "06ef92126debd9d4c330890f1abae15e32bdcd4c",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ba5201f7_c1df3e38",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/test_v3_filters.py",
        "patchSetId": 33
      },
      "lineNbr": 375,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-01-11T20:45:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We probably don\u0027t need to have a docstring that is exactly the same as the test name, but instead we could use the docstring to explain *why* the behavior in the test is the way it is (if needed of course).",
      "revId": "06ef92126debd9d4c330890f1abae15e32bdcd4c",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
